## Pictures as tokens

<At levels="simple">

A transformer doesn't care what its tokens stand for; it just needs a row of vectors (<ChapterRef to="embeddings" />). A
picture can become one by being cut into square **patches**, each turned into a vector, just like a
token. This is the *vision transformer*.<Cite id="dosovitskiy2021" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A transformer doesn't care what its tokens stand for. All it needs is a row of vectors (<ChapterRef to="embeddings" />). Text
becomes that row by splitting into tokens. A picture can do the same by being cut into square
**patches**, each turned into a vector by multiplying its pixel values by learned weights, plus a
position, just like a token. The rest is the familiar stack of blocks. This is the *vision
transformer*.<Cite id="dosovitskiy2021" />

</At>

Here it is with CLIP ViT-B/32, a real model, on simple pictures drawn for this page. It cuts each
224×224-pixel picture into a 7×7 grid of 32×32-pixel patches: 49 "tokens". Pick a patch:

<PatchCutter />

Notice that patches of plain background all turn into the same vector, the way the same word always
starts as the same embedding.

<At levels="deep">

### A patch, in numbers

Each 32×32 patch has 3 colors per pixel: 32 × 32 × 3 = 3,072 numbers. One learned 3,072 × 768
weight matrix turns it into 768 numbers, the same matrix for every patch, and a position vector is
added. One extra learned vector goes at the front, a *class token*, so the transformer reads 50
vectors, and at the end the class token's vector stands for the whole picture.<Cite id="dosovitskiy2021" />
CLIP then maps that vector, and the text side's summary of a caption, into a shared space of 512
numbers.

</At>

## A shared map for words and pictures

<At levels="simple">

CLIP was trained on 400 million pictures with their captions from the internet, learning to put
each picture close to its own caption.<Cite id="radford2021" /> So words and pictures land in one shared space. Pick some
words and see which pictures land closest:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

CLIP was trained on 400 million pictures paired with their captions from the internet. It has two
parts, one that reads pictures and one that reads text, and training pushed each picture's vector
close to its own caption's vector and away from the others.<Cite id="radford2021" /> The result is a
single space where words and pictures can be compared. Pick some words and see which pictures land
closest:

</At>

<PictureMatch />

For each of the 12 exact descriptions, the right picture comes first. Vaguer words are harder:
"something round" barely prefers a circle.

<At levels="deep">

### Comparing a caption with pictures

For each caption, CLIP takes the cosine similarity with each picture's vector and multiplies by a
learned temperature, 100 for this model, before the softmax. The similarities themselves sit close
together: "a red circle" scores 0.271 with the red circle and 0.268 with the red square. Multiplied
by 100, that 0.003 gap gives the circle 42% and the square 32%. In training, each batch held 32,768
picture–caption pairs: every picture had to pick out its own caption from all 32,768, and every
caption its picture.<Cite id="radford2021" />

</At>

## Chat models that see

<At levels="simple">

A <GlossaryPopover term="multimodal">multimodal</GlossaryPopover> chat model combines the two
ideas: a vision model turns the picture into vectors, and the language model reads them alongside
your words.<Cite id="liu2023" /> To the language model, the picture is just more tokens.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A <GlossaryPopover term="multimodal">multimodal</GlossaryPopover> chat model puts the two ideas
together. A vision model like CLIP's turns the picture into patch vectors, a small layer converts
those into the same kind of vectors as text tokens, and the language model reads them in its
context alongside your words.<Cite id="liu2023" /> To the language model, the picture is just more
tokens.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A vision transformer cuts a picture into patches and treats each patch like a token.
- CLIP puts pictures and captions into one shared space, so words can find matching pictures.
- Chat models that see turn a picture into tokens and put them in the context next to the text.

<At levels="deep">

- A patch's 3,072 pixel values become 768 numbers; CLIP scales its similarities by 100 before the
  softmax.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** these models read pictures. How do image generators make them?
